  "version": "0.1.0"
## remux-audio --help
remux-audio — lay music/SFX stems under the narration of a rendered MP4 (video copied, never re-encoded)

Usage: remux-audio <video.mp4> --out <file.mp4> [--music <file>] [--sfx <file>] [options]

The input's audio is the narration (or pass --narration). It is levelled to --narration-lufs,
the music bed to --music-lufs and ducked under the voice by a sidechain compressor, and the
SFX stem is added at --sfx-db. Stems are timeline-aligned from 0 and trimmed or padded to the
video length. The video stream is copied: its md5 (-map 0:v -c copy -f md5) is unchanged.
Run each pass (music, then music + SFX) from the narration-only review MP4.

Options:
  -o, --out <file.mp4>         Output MP4 (must differ from the input)
      --music <file>           Music bed stem (any audio file ffmpeg reads)
      --sfx <file>             SFX stem, already placed on the timeline
      --narration <file>       Narration source (default: the input video's audio)
      --narration-lufs <LUFS>  Narration loudness (default: -16)
      --music-lufs <LUFS>      Music bed loudness before ducking (default: -28)
      --sfx-db <dB>            Gain applied to the SFX stem (default: 0)
      --no-duck                Do not duck the music under the narration
      --fade-ms <ms>           Music fade-out at the end (default: 1000)
      --levels <file.json>     Also write the levels report (LUFS, true peak, ducking) here
      --json                   Print the levels report as JSON
  -h, --help                   Show this help and exit
      --version                Print the plugin version and exit

Examples:
  remux-audio review/review.mp4 --music audio/music.wav -o review/review-music.mp4
  remux-audio review/review.mp4 --music audio/music.wav --sfx audio/sfx.wav -o review/review-mix.mp4 --levels review/levels.json
exit 0
